WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.000
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.167
is an Academy Award-winning actress you know

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:05.434
from her work in the films

00:00:05.434 --> 00:00:07.434
"Walk the Line" and "Legally Blonde,"

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:08.868
as well as the hit series

00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:10.567
"Big Little Lies" and "The Morning Show."

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:13.501
She stars in the new movie "Your Place or Mine,"

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:15.200
which premieres on Netflix February 10th.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.033
Please welcome back to the show Reese Witherspoon, everybody.

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:19.968
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:29.434
♪♪

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:35.968
♪♪

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:38.067
I'm so happy to have you here!

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.968
-Look at Fred playing the drums. -Isn't it?

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:43.100
He's just a man of many trades. -And perfume.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.868
He has his own man perfume. -Yeah. Well, I don't think

00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:47.968
-It's amazing. -it's just for men. Right, Fred?

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:50.234
-Can I have some? -Yes, it's for everybody.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:52.033
[ Laughter ] -Okay, so, okay, okay.

00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.534
He's always for everybody. -It's unisex.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:55.701
-Yeah, absolutely. -That's so good!

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:57.834
-[ Laughs ] It's so nice to have you here

00:00:57.834 --> 00:00:59.634
and I'm very excited about "The Morning Show."

00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:01.734
You just wrapped Season 3. -Yes!

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:03.367
-And I'm very excited about that.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.567
And I have to imagine, is this the longest thing

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:08.033
you've ever worked on? -It actually is.

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.067
This has been five years of my life,

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:11.667
three seasons, ten episodes each.

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:14.234
Jennifer Aniston and I were talking about it the other day.

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:17.400
It's like making five movies, so it's like we made 15 movies.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.601
[ Laughter ] -Yeah, it's very impressive.

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:20.133
-Which was fun. -Fifteen movies is really, yeah.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:23.467
-We made 15 movies together. -And you added Jon Hamm

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:25.334
to the cast this year, one of our favorites here.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:26.601
-He's so great. -That must be a...

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:28.067
very easy person to be around.

00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:30.934
-So talented, so smart, and, off the top of his head,

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:33.701
really funny, just comes up with really funny stuff.

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:35.701
-I'm deeply -- I'm always deeply angry

00:01:35.701 --> 00:01:39.067
at how funny Jon Hamm is because people who look like Jon Hamm

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:40.634
shouldn't also be funny. [ Laughter ]

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:41.968
-He's really -- Yeah, it's true.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.334
-Yeah. -It's a real drag.

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:44.734
-Yeah, he's really, really funny.

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:46.701
-Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:49.200
So, I didn't remember this until I saw the photo,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.501
but you and Jennifer Aniston worked together before.

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:54.901
Twenty years ago, you played her younger sister on "Friends."

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.367
-I was her younger on "Friends."

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:59.734
[ Cheering and applause ] -Now...what --

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:01.767
-Yes, and the funny storyline was

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:03.901
I started dating Ross because she and Ross were

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:05.367
"on a break." -Yeah.

00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:07.367
I do recall hearing that at some point

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:09.334
about the two of them.

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.567
-And she gets really jealous and she's like,

00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:15.133
"You can't date Ross," and I was like, "I can't?

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:17.334
The only thing I can't have is dairy."

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:19.534
[ Laughter ] -Whoo!

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:21.667
I still remember the line. -You're still off-book.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:23.567
-I still --

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.701
Their writing was so good on that show,

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.100
so it's really memorable.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.767
I have all sorts of funny, famous "Friends" lines

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:31.234
that are stuck in my head. Don't y'all?

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:32.834
-Yeah, of course. -Everybody has a favorite.

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:34.133
-Do you remember what it felt like

00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:36.000
to walk onto that set? Because, at the time,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.434
it was already a phenomenon.

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:40.000
-Oh, my gosh, yeah. So, I was terrified.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.334
I was out of my body. I was -- My body was there

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:45.067
and then my head and my brain was somewhere else.

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:46.701
I just had a baby. -Wow!

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.968
-Yeah. So, that's how I know it was 23 years ago,

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.934
because, now, my baby is 23.

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:54.133
But walking into Central Perk... -Yeah.

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:55.534
-...that main coffee place

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:56.834
and sitting on the couch, I was like,

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:57.934
"Oh, my God, I can't believe this is happening.

00:02:57.934 --> 00:02:58.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:00.734
Oh, my God, there's Joey. Oh, my God."

00:03:00.734 --> 00:03:02.834
And then wait! Joey said to me,

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.133
"How you doing?"

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:06.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.100
-He didn't just say that to everybody.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:11.834
[ Applause ]

00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:14.167
-You're right. No, but it was so fun.

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:17.234
It was so -- I was terrified, but Aniston was so sweet to me.

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:19.133
She was like, "They don't care if you mess up.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:20.734
They actually like it better when you mess up."

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:22.033
-Oh, that's a very nice thing to hear

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:23.701
before you do any bit of acting.

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:26.067
-Any live show or whatever. It wasn't live,

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:29.033
but it was recorded in front of this many people.

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:30.567
Maybe more. -Exactly. It's a big deal.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:33.300
I mean, I didn't do a ton of sitcom work,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.901
but the first thing I ever did was in a sitcom,

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:37.234
the first line I had on television.

00:03:37.234 --> 00:03:40.300
-Hold on. I think I was on your very first "SNL."

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.834
-You were on my very first "SNL," yeah.

00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:43.300
-Okay, so, I was the host of "SNL"...

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.567
-Yep.

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:45.968
-...on your very first day on "SNL."

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:47.801
-That is absolutely -- September 29, 2001.

00:03:47.801 --> 00:03:50.267
How about that? [ Cheering and applause ]

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:53.133
Reese Witherspoon and -- -I remember.

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:55.200
-And Alicia Keys. -Yeah, Alicia Keys. Oh, my gosh.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.834
And then Amy Poehler's first day, too.

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:58.434
-Yep, it was Amy Poehler's first day.

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:00.467
-And I'll never forget meeting you and Amy and thinking,

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:02.033
"Oh, my God, these people are so talented.

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:03.801
This is going to be so good."

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:06.067
-That was a rare case, where, no matter how nervous you were,

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:07.734
I promise I was more nervous on that day.

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:08.767
[ Laughter ] -Aww.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:10.100
-It was a trip. -But what a dream,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.968
what a dream, that you've had this incredible journey.

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:13.334
-Oh, thank you.

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:15.400
I'm glad that we're making this about me, I really do.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.801
[ Laughter ] -Well.

00:04:16.801 --> 00:04:18.534
I do want to talk about your --

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:20.767
Well, you are so loved by so many people

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:22.000
-Oh, my God. -and the fact that like --

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.300
Like, I saw Paul at --

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.200
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.868
You are. You're just really deeply respected.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.601
Everybody finds you so hysterical,

00:04:30.601 --> 00:04:32.767
but also you're just a genuinely nice human,

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:34.601
so, like, Fred comes and plays the drums

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:37.000
because you're just a genuinely great person.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.968
-This is so -- And it's so hard for me to say this right now

00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:41.834
because you're so mean off-camera.

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:44.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.434
But, no, it's a real trip when you --

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:48.567
-I just don't want you to tell the truth about how awful I was

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.000
on "SNL." You're like, "Oh, God."

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.234
-No, God, it was wonderful.

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:52.968
-It was tough. -I mean, it was also, you know,

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:54.501
if we're really talking about that day,

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:55.801
the craziest thing about that show is

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:57.300
it was two weeks after 9/11. -Yeah.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.734
-It was, you know, this insane time here,

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.300
and it was also really grounding

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.734
to be able to go to work

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.734
and actually do something for people.

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:06.901
-It was terrifying. Yeah, it was a terrifying time

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:09.934
and we were in New York City and it was right after 9/11

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.868
and we were like -- and Lorne was so adamant

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:13.400
that people needed to laugh. -Yes.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.100
-And I think he was right.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.934
And he brought all these firefighters on

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:18.601
and Paul Simon was there. It was amazing.

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:19.634
-It was. And I felt --

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:21.367
[ Applause ] Yeah, it was a really --

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:24.434
[ Cheering ] -You did a really great job.

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:27.601
-And, when you left "SNL," when I left,

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:29.267
there was a producer named Kenny Aymong

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:30.734
who used to do this wonderful thing where he would give you

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:32.934
the ticket of your first show and your last show, framed...

00:05:32.934 --> 00:05:35.167
-Aww. -...so I've got my Reese Witherspoon ticket

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:36.767
-Oh, my God! -on my office wall right now.

00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:38.567
-That's awesome. -I think about you every day...

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:39.801
-Oh, my gosh. -...very fondly.

00:05:39.801 --> 00:05:41.601
Not in a creepy way, just like -- yeah.

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:43.434
[ Laughter ] Just like a normal, healthy,

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:44.601
the way people should think

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:46.033
about Reese Witherspoon every day.

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:48.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.501
-But, you know, I feel really lucky

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:51.334
to be part of people's lives for so long.

00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:54.133
And the fact that people watch my movies and my TV shows

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:56.968
and it's like that's a big gift, to have people enjoy your work

00:05:56.968 --> 00:05:59.501
and have a career where people watched you for so long

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:00.901
and I feel really personally about it.

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:03.434
I just ran into a chocolate store

00:06:03.434 --> 00:06:05.367
because I'm, like, trying to buy chocolates

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:09.300
for my kids for Valentine's Day and this girl goes,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.234
"Oh, my God! Are you Reese Witherspoon?!"

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.067
I was like, "Yeah! [ Laughter ]

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:16.067
I am!" And she's like -- she's like, "Oh, my God!

00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:17.534
I'm an acting student. What should I do?"

00:06:17.534 --> 00:06:19.267
And I was like, "Okay, first, you do this, then you do that.

00:06:19.267 --> 00:06:21.534
Then you audition your butt off.

00:06:21.534 --> 00:06:23.000
You're not going to get every job,

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.467
but just work your tail off." And then I thought,

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:26.901
"I'm so, so, so lucky to have people like that in my life."

00:06:26.901 --> 00:06:28.934
I'm really lucky. I'm lucky. -Well, it's also -- it's so nice

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.334
to use those moments

00:06:30.334 --> 00:06:32.701
because, again, you have them and sometimes they happen

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:34.601
when you maybe don't have the time or focus

00:06:34.601 --> 00:06:36.100
you want to give to a person, but it's so --

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.000
Anytime you do -- Anytime you actually take a breath

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.601
and realize, oh, you would be nothing if it wasn't

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:41.367
for people like this who actually care about your work.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:43.300
-Yeah, I'd be nothing without -- -To give them something back.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.334
-Like, yeah. I have a movie coming on Netflix.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:47.067
It's my very first Netflix movie.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:48.701
-Very exciting.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:50.367 --> 00:06:53.334
-And I just -- I thought, you know, I was like --

00:06:53.334 --> 00:06:54.834
I really -- I set a goal.

00:06:54.834 --> 00:06:56.334
I was like, "I really want to be on a Netflix movie,"

00:06:56.334 --> 00:06:57.467
because I think it's so important

00:06:57.467 --> 00:06:59.234
that so many people can't leave their house.

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:00.567
-Yep. -They can't go places.

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:02.501
They're taking care of kids. They have older parents.

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:04.267
They have -- They work their tails off,

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:05.801
and then they're exhausted, and all they want to do

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:08.400
is just sit on the couch with potato chips like this.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.133
So I'm really excited. It's a really great, positive movie.

00:07:12.133 --> 00:07:14.200
-I want to talk about it after this break.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.767
I also want to say, to me, it's deeply funny that you --

00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:20.567
When that person at the chocolate shop saw you,

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:23.200
you were doing something very out of a rom-com,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.834
which was --

00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:26.234
The idea of, like, meeting Reese Witherspoon

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:28.200
when she runs into a chocolate shop.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.367
-Right. I'm running. -To buy candy for Halloween.

00:07:30.367 --> 00:07:32.033
Not for Halloween. Valentine's Day.

00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:33.334
-I was actually -- I was late for your show

00:07:33.334 --> 00:07:34.767
because I was buying chocolate.

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:36.334
I was like, "Pull over!"

00:07:36.334 --> 00:07:38.501
And, like, with the full big box, it's a heart.

00:07:38.501 --> 00:07:40.367
It was fully a rom-com moment. -I love it.

00:07:40.367 --> 00:07:43.567
Well, I mean, it comes natural to some people, yeah.

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:45.267
I want to ask about the movie.

00:07:45.267 --> 00:07:48.133
We'll be right back with more from Reese Witherspoon.

